From ecd05da2614b5f8f12919c8a3b6d44e7ea4b4ebe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Walter Dal Mut <walter.dalmut@corley.it>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 20:09:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 14/61] ARM: dts: rockchip: rk3506: mark the OP-TEE carve-out
 no-map

The node carried a TODO(verify) saying that no-map is the idiomatic form
for a secure-world carve-out but was untested on this SoC, so the vendor
form was kept.

It is now tested, and the answer is stronger than expected: the range is
not just reserved, it is unreachable from normal world. A bare read from
the U-Boot prompt, which runs in normal world, aborts:

    => md 0x4000 4
    00004000:data abort
    pc : 00254086  lr : 00254029
    ### ERROR ### Please RESET the board ###

Without no-map the range stays in the kernel linear map, so the kernel
holds mappings for memory that faults on access. Add no-map and close the
TODO.

While here, record on ramoops@83000 why its address is not a problem even
though it sits below the 2 MiB floor that linux,usable-memory-range
establishes in the board dts: capping memblock removes the range from the
allocator, not from reserved-memory. Verified on the board - pstore comes
up and uses that region.

Signed-off-by: Walter Dal Mut <walter.dalmut@corley.it>
(cherry picked from commit 0f255fc56c22a134ea6592a7905656ae5387dfa7)

diff --git a/arch/arm/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3506.dtsi b/arch/arm/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3506.dtsi
index 253a9c66c..44d1286d6 100644
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3506.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3506.dtsi
@@ -77,13 +77,21 @@ reserved-memory {
 		 * from here, so handing this range to the page allocator kills
 		 * SMP bring-up and PSCI system-off.
 		 *
-		 * TODO(verify): the vendor node (rk3502.dtsi:335) has no
-		 * "no-map", so this range stays in the kernel linear map.
-		 * "no-map" is the idiomatic mainline form for a secure-world
-		 * carve-out but is untested on this SoC; kept as vendor.
+		 * no-map because the range is not merely reserved, it is
+		 * unreachable: the memory firewall rejects normal-world
+		 * access, and even a read aborts. Measured from the U-Boot
+		 * prompt, which also runs in normal world:
+		 *
+		 *     => md 0x4000 4
+		 *     00004000:data abort
+		 *
+		 * The vendor node (rk3502.dtsi:335) omits no-map and so leaves
+		 * the range in the kernel linear map, i.e. mappings that must
+		 * never be touched.
 		 */
 		trust@0 {
 			reg = <0x0 0x62000>;
+			no-map;
 		};
 
 		/*
@@ -91,6 +99,12 @@ trust@0 {
 		 * console was up. Vendor also sets boot-log-size and
 		 * boot-log-count (rk3502.dtsi:352) - those are Rockchip
 		 * extensions with no mainline binding, dropped here.
+		 *
+		 * The address is below the 2 MiB floor that
+		 * linux,usable-memory-range establishes in the board dts, and
+		 * that is fine: capping memblock removes the range from the
+		 * allocator, not from reserved-memory. Verified on the board,
+		 * pstore comes up and uses this region.
 		 */
 		ramoops@83000 {
 			compatible = "ramoops";
